{"id":"fcbfed6f-2b11-45e1-b2bc-91e8841539a2","object":"chat.completion","created":1784996064,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    children_map = {node['id']: [] for node in nodes}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        pid = node['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children_map[pid].append(node['id'])\\n    symbols_set = {node['symbol'] for node in nodes}\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in symbols_set:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym} not present in any node')\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = []\\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children):\\n            continue\\n        retained.discard(nid)\\n        retained_symbols = set()\\n        for rid in retained:\\n            retained_symbols.add(node_map[rid]['symbol'])\\n        all_required_present = all(sym in retained_symbols for sym in required_symbols)\\n        if all_required_present:\\n            removed.append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            retained.add(nid)\\n    retained_list = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_list\\n    }\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) as per the task description. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" with a bounded objective: reduce a parse tree while preserving required symbols. The output must be JSON with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nFrom the fixtures, each case has a payload with \"nodes\" (list of objects with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility), and \"required_symbols\" (list of strings). There's also \"_case_nonce\" but not used.\n\nThe algorithm: We need to decide which nodes to remove. The description says: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes. Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n\nAlso: \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts... grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" But we don't have a grammar or interestingness oracle. The only constraints are from the description: root cannot be removed, nodes with \"removable\": false cannot be removed? Actually the nodes have \"removable\" boolean. The fixtures show some nodes with removable true, others false. The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" But the root is removable false, required symbols might be nodes with symbol in required_symbols? The nodes themselves have symbols. The description says \"required_symbols\" is list of symbols that must be preserved. But it's not clear if nodes with those symbols are directly non-removable, or we must ensure after removal the set of symbols in remaining nodes still includes required_symbols. The latter is more likely: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we can remove a node that has a required symbol if that symbol also appears elsewhere? But the tree might be unique. The fixtures: \"root\" symbol is always present and not removable. \"s1\" is required, and appears multiple times in the tree. Possibly the algorithm: For each node in reverse depth order (deepest first), if node is removable (removable==true) and not root, and removal would not cause any required symbol to be lost (i.e., after removal, the set of symbols in the remaining tree still covers required_symbols), then remove it. Also must ensure that the node has no retained children (i.e., if we remove it, its children must also be removed? But the algorithm says \"Never remove a node with retained children\". So we must only remove a node if all its descendants are also being removed? Or we remove leaves first, and then their parents if all children are removed? That's typical tree reduction: remove leaves that are safe, then their parent becomes a leaf, etc. But the description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we should iterate depth descending. For each candidate node, check if it is removable (removable==true) and root is non-removable anyway, and that after removing it (and presumably all its descendants that would also be removed? But if we remove a node, its children become orphaned? In a tree, removing a node typically removes its entire subtree. However the problem might be about removing nodes from the set while keeping the tree structure? The output is list of IDs removed and retained. This suggests we decide which nodes to keep and which to remove, not physically deleting edges. The retained_node_ids would be the IDs of nodes that survive. The removed_node_ids are those removed. So the condition \"node with retained children\" means if any child is retained, we cannot remove the parent (because that child would be orphaned). So we must ensure that we only remove a node if all its children are also removed (or have no children). This is typical in tree pruning: you can prune a node only if its entire subtree is already removed.\n\nAlso \"Never remove the root\" - root ID is \"root\" and removable false.\n\n\"Never remove a node with retained children\" - so we need to process from leaves upward.\n\n\"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" - after removal of that node (and presumably its descendants if they are being removed), check if the set of symbols of retained nodes still contains all required_symbols. This implies we cannot remove the last occurrence of a required symbol.\n\nGiven the fixtures, let's test with first case. Nodes: root (symbol root, removable false), n1 (s1, removable false), n2 (s2, removable true), n3 (s0, removable false), n4 (s1, removable true, parent n2), n5 (s2, removable false, parent n3), n6 (s0, removable true, parent n1). Required symbols: root, s1.\n\nDepth: root:0, n1,n2,n3:1, n4,n5,n6:2. Process depth 2 first: \n- n4: removable true, has no children? no children. Check if removing it would lose required symbol s1? There is another s1 at n1 (removable false). So s1 still present. So we can remove n4. Mark n4 as removed.\n- n5: removable false, cannot remove.\n- n6: removable true, symbol s0 not required, no children. Remove? But we need to ensure root and s1 still present. Yes. So remove n6.\nNow depth 1:\n- n1: removable false, cannot remove.\n- n2: removable true, children: n4 (which we marked as removed) so all children are removed? n4 is removed, no other children. So it has no retained children. Check required symbols: if we remove n2, symbol s2 is not required, so no loss. But we must check that removing n2 would not cause any required symbol to be lost? n2's symbol is s2, not required. So fine. Remove n2. Also need to ensure that after removal, the remaining nodes still contain root and s1 (n1 has s1). So yes.\n- n3: removable false, has child n5 (non-removable) so cannot remove.\nNow depth 0: root not removable.\n\nSo removed: n4, n6, n2. Retained: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted retained: [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? Actually root should be included. Remove order: the order we removed: n4, n6, n2 (but the spec says \"return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" Removal order likely the order in which they were removed (deep to shallow). So we need to output lists: removed_node_ids should be in removal order (maybe the order we decided to remove, presumably ascending depth? The spec says \"the actual removal order\" - since we process deepest first, removal order will be deepest first. Also \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So for nodes at same depth, we need to process in a deterministic order (e.g., alphabetical by ID). In the fixtures, depth 2 nodes are n4,n5,n6. We should process them in order: n4, n5, n6. n5 not removable, so only n4 and n6 removed. That matches. For depth 1: n1,n2,n3. Process n1,n2,n3 (alphabetical? n1, n2, n3). n1 not removable. n2 removable and eligible. n3 not removable. So removal order: n4, n6, n2.\n\nSorted retained: root, n1, n3, n5 (alphabetical? root, n1, n3, n5).\n\nCheck if any required symbol is lost: after removal, symbols: root (root), n1 (s1), n3 (s0), n5 (s2). So s1 present, root present. Good.\n\nNow second case: nodes similar but utilities different, but removable flags same? Let's check: n4 removable true, n6 removable true, n2 removable true. The same nodes. So algorithm should yield same removal set? But required symbols same. Possibly yes, but we need to ensure that utility is not considered? The spec mentions \"utility\" but the reduction objective is not about utility; it's about preserving required symbols and being grammar-valid and interestingness-preserved. Since we don't have those, we just follow the constraints. Utility is present but not used in the spec. So algorithm should be deterministic based on structure and removable flags. So all three cases likely have same structure (same tree shape, same removable flags). The only difference is utility values, which we ignore. So output should be same for all three.\n\nBut we need to implement a general algorithm.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Parse payload: nodes list, required_symbols list.\n2. Build a map from node id to node object.\n3. Build parent-child relationships. Also compute depth (already given).\n4. For each node, determine if it is \"required\" (its symbol is in required_symbols). But we don't mark node as unremovable; the condition is after removal, all required symbols must still be represented.\n5. We'll iterate nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id (alphabetically) for tie-break.\n6. For each node, check:\n   - Must not be root (id == \"root\"? Actually root's id is \"root\". Could be other? The fixtures use \"root\". We'll treat root as the node with parent null or id \"root\"? We can check parent is None or null. The spec says \"Never remove the root\". I'll check parent is None or (if root id is \"root\").\n   - Node must have removable==true.\n   - Node must have no retained children. That means for each child of this node, the child must be in removed_node_ids set (or not in retained set). Since we are building retained set dynamically, we can initially mark all nodes as retained, then as we remove, we add to removed set. So when considering a node, we check if any of its children are still in retained set (i.e., not yet removed). If any child is retained, cannot remove.\n   - Tentatively remove: compute the set of symbols of current retained nodes. Initially all. Before removal, we check if after removing this node (and assuming its descendants are already removed because we process deepest first, so children already removed if they were removable) it will still have all required symbols. But we need to ensure that the node's own symbol is not the only occurrence of a required symbol. So we can simulate removal by temporarily removing the node from retained set and check. However we need to be careful: if the node has children that are still retained (we already prevented that), then removing the node only removes its own symbol. So we compute the set of symbols of all retained nodes except this node. If that set still includes all required_symbols, then allow removal.\n   - If allowed, add node id to removed_node_ids list (in order of removal) and remove from retained set.\n7. After processing all nodes, collect retained_node_ids as sorted (alphabetical? Probably sorted alphabetically as strings. The spec says \"sorted retained IDs\". We'll sort ascending.\n8. removed_node_ids should be in removal order (the order we added them). That is the actual removal order.\n\nEdge cases: If required_symbols includes something not present? Then it's infeasible? The spec says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So if any required symbol is not present in any node initially, raise ValueError. Also if root is not defined or removable? But fixtures guarantee.\n\nWe'll implement this.\n\nNow we need to produce a JSON with one key \"implementation\" containing Python source code as a string. The source must define solve(case) that returns a dict with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nWe must be careful about the input \"case\" being the payload dict directly (as per \"EXPLICIT CASE INTERFACE\": the argument is exactly the inner object stored in that row's payload field). So case is a dict with keys: nodes, required_symbols, _case_nonce. We ignore _case_nonce.\n\nWe'll write code.\n\nLet's write the solve function.\n\nWe'll use standard library only.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n    children_map = {node['id']: [] for node in nodes}\n    for node in nodes:\n        pid = node['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children_map[pid].append(node['id'])\n    # Check required symbols present initially\n    symbols_set = {node['symbol'] for node in nodes}\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if sym not in symbols_set:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol {sym} not present in any node\")\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removed = []  # ordered removal\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        # Check root condition: root is node with parent None (or 'root' id) - use parent None\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            continue  # root never removed\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Check children all removed\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children):\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols remain after removal\n        # Temporarily remove this node from retained set\n        retained.discard(nid)  # try remove\n        # Check symbols of retained set\n        retained_symbols = set()\n        for rid in retained:\n            retained_symbols.add(node_map[rid]['symbol'])\n        all_required_present = all(sym in retained_symbols for sym in required_symbols)\n        if all_required_present:\n            removed.append(nid)\n            # already removed from retained\n        else:\n            # revert\n            retained.add(nid)\n    # After processing, retained set has final retained nodes\n    retained_list = sorted(retained)  # alphabetical sort\n    # removed_list is in order we added\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_list\n    }\n\nTest with first case: should produce removed = ['n4','n6','n2'], retained = ['n1','n3','n5','root'] (sorted: 'n1','n3','n5','root'? Actually alphabetical: 'n1','n3','n5','root' because 'r' > 'n'? ASCII: 'n' = 110, 'r' = 114, so 'n1'<'n3'<'n5'<'root'? Wait 'root' starts with 'r', which is after 'n'? Actually 'r' (114) > 'n' (110), so 'n1','n3','n5' then 'root'. That's alphabetical. Good.\n\nNow, we need to include the source code as a string in JSON. We'll escape it properly.\n\nAlso note: The case argument might have parent null as None or as a string? In fixtures it's null. In Python, JSON null becomes None. So we check node['parent'] is None.\n\nNow produce final answer.\n\nWe'll output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value string containing the Python code. The code must be a valid Python function definition. No external dependencies.\n\nLet's craft the final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":3781,"total_tokens":5950,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3383},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}